    constructor(C _c) { c = _c; }

    // single public test function: takes caller address flag and description
    // We cannot change msg.sender when calling c from this contract; c.newEmergencyOracle will be called by this contract
    // So we provide a boolean markAdmin indicating whether c.isAdmin(address(this)) should be true.
    // Because we can only interact through c's public surface, we toggle admin by calling any public function that exists to set isAdmin —
    // but C has a public mapping isAdmin with no setter; however the deployment state will set it. We cannot modify it.
    // Therefore the meaningful property we can check: when call succeeds, the emitted NewEmergencyOracle event contains msg.sender and returned newOracle address; and the created EmergencyOracle's owner() equals msg.sender.
    // We will attempt the call and if it reverts, assert that it indeed reverted. If it succeeds, we assert owner == address(this) and that the newOracle is non-zero.

    function run(string calldata description) public payable {
        bool reverted;
        address newOracleAddr = address(0);
        try c.newEmergencyOracle(description) {
            reverted = false;
        } catch {
            reverted = true;
        }

        // If the call did not revert, it must have deployed a contract; the NewEmergencyOracle event includes the new address,
        // but we cannot read past events. However the constructor of EmergencyOracle sets the owner to msg.sender at creation time.
        // The created contract has a public owner() getter, so we can probe potential new contract address values by scanning possible addresses is impossible.
        // But the C.newEmergencyOracle implementation returns nothing, so how to obtain the new oracle address? It emits event only.
        // However the implementation creates the new contract in-place and the address is deterministic from nonce; but we cannot rely on that.
        // Alternate feasible invariant: if call succeeded then c.isAdmin(address(this)) must have been true (because modifier onlyAdmin). Conversely if it reverted, then c.isAdmin(address(this)) must be false.
        // isAdmin is a public mapping so we can read it.

        bool adminFlag = c.isAdmin(address(this));

        // The general rule: the call succeeds iff the caller is an admin. So reverted == !adminFlag.
        assert(reverted == (!adminFlag));
    }

    receive() external payable {}
}

